      "role": "user",
      "content": "You are a culinary science expert. Provide detailed, scientifically grounded explanations regarding fermentation and baking chemistry.\n\nHey, I'm a professional baker and I'm hitting a wall with my sourdough starter stability. Lately, I've been seeing these massive, unpredictable pH swings during the bulk fermentation stage, and it's completely ruining my oven spring. I need to know if I should be looking at ambient temperature fluctuations in the proofing area or if I need to adjust my flour profile. \n\nSpecifically, can you explain the electrochemical relationship between ambient temperature and the rate of acid production in a sourdough culture? Also, how does increasing the protein content in my flour help buffer those pH spikes? I need the actual chemistry behind this so I can stabilize my batches. Thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator",
